A small recursive version can find the root position by scanning only the current inorder range. The next preorder value is always the root of that range. Once its position is found, the range is split into left and right parts.

This keeps the code short and does not need a map. The trade-off is the extra scan at each level, so a badly unbalanced tree can take quadratic time. Empty input is handled before reading `preorder[0]`.

```go
func buildTree(preorder []int, inorder []int) *TreeNode {
    preorderIndex := 0

    var build func(int, int) *TreeNode
    build = func(left, right int) *TreeNode {
        if left > right {
            return nil
        }

        value := preorder[preorderIndex]
        preorderIndex++

        middle := left
        for middle <= right && inorder[middle] != value {
            middle++
        }

        node := &TreeNode{data: value}
        node.left = build(left, middle-1)
        node.right = build(middle+1, right)
        return node
    }

    if len(preorder) == 0 || len(inorder) == 0 {
        return nil
    }
    return build(0, len(inorder)-1)
}
```

An edge case is a tree with only right children. Each call then gets an empty left range and continues with the right range, so no special tree shape code is needed.

Time complexity: O(n^2) in the worst case because the current inorder range may be scanned repeatedly.
Space complexity: O(n) for the recursion depth in a skewed tree.

Kept the index search inside the current range instead of using a map. The first unused preorder value becomes the root, and a short loop finds that value in inorder. The number of values before it tells us exactly which range belongs to the left child.

This is useful as a basic version because the preorder index and range boundaries make the matching clear. It reads both input vectors without changing them.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
    TreeNode* makeTree(vector<int>& preorder, vector<int>& inorder,
                       int& preorderIndex, int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        int value = preorder[preorderIndex++];
        int middle = left;
        while (middle <= right && inorder[middle] != value) {
            ++middle;
        }

        TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(value);
        root->left = makeTree(preorder, inorder, preorderIndex,
                              left, middle - 1);
        root->right = makeTree(preorder, inorder, preorderIndex,
                               middle + 1, right);
        return root;
    }

public:
    TreeNode* buildTree(vector<int>& preorder, vector<int>& inorder) {
        if (preorder.empty() || inorder.empty()) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        int preorderIndex = 0;
        return makeTree(preorder, inorder, preorderIndex,
                        0, static_cast<int>(inorder.size()) - 1);
    }
};
```

For a one-node input, the loop finds the root at the only index and both recursive ranges become empty. That is also why the `left > right` check is enough for leaf nodes.

Time complexity: O(n^2) in the worst case due to the range scan at every recursive level.
Space complexity: O(n) for recursion in the worst tree shape.

Preorder always gives the next root, while inorder tells where that root separates the two children. I store every inorder value's position in an unordered map, so finding the split is quick.

The helper receives only an inorder range. A shared preorder index moves forward as nodes are created. The left range is built before the right range, which follows the order in preorder.

A single node and an empty input are both covered by the range check. The input vectors are only read and are not changed.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
    unordered_map<int, int> inorderPosition;
    int preorderIndex = 0;

    TreeNode* build(vector<int>& preorder, int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        int value = preorder[preorderIndex++];
        TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(value);
        int middle = inorderPosition[value];

        root->left = build(preorder, left, middle - 1);
        root->right = build(preorder, middle + 1, right);
        return root;
    }

public:
    TreeNode* buildTree(vector<int>& preorder, vector<int>& inorder) {
        inorderPosition.clear();
        preorderIndex = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(inorder.size()); ++i) {
            inorderPosition[inorder[i]] = i;
        }

        return build(preorder, 0, static_cast<int>(inorder.size()) - 1);
    }
};
```

Dry run for `[3,9,20,15,7]` and `[9,3,15,20,7]`: `3` is created first and splits inorder at index 1. The left range creates `9`. The remaining preorder value `20` starts the right range, which then creates `15` and `7` below it.

Time complexity: O(n), since each node is processed once and map lookup is average O(1).
Space complexity: O(n) for the map and recursion stack.

This version avoids recursion. A stack stores nodes whose right side may still need to be built. When the stack top matches the next inorder value, that node is finished, so the next preorder node must become a right child. Otherwise, it becomes a left child.

The empty array check matters because there is no root to place on the stack. The arrays are not modified.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class Solution {
    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {
        if (preorder.length == 0) {
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[0]);
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(root);
        int inorderIndex = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < preorder.length; i++) {
            TreeNode current = stack.peek();

            if (current.data != inorder[inorderIndex]) {
                current.left = new TreeNode(preorder[i]);
                stack.push(current.left);
            } else {
                while (!stack.isEmpty()
                        && inorderIndex < inorder.length
                        && stack.peek().data == inorder[inorderIndex]) {
                    current = stack.pop();
                    inorderIndex++;
                }

                current.right = new TreeNode(preorder[i]);
                stack.push(current.right);
            }
        }

        return root;
    }
}
```

For the sample input, the stack first grows through `3` and `9`. After `9` matches inorder, it is popped, then `20` is attached on the right of `3`. The same check places `15` and `7` under `20`.

Time complexity: O(n), with each node pushed and popped at most once.
Space complexity: O(n) for the stack.

## Solution
```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {
        HashMap<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for(int i=0; i<inorder.length; i++){
            map.put(inorder[i], i);
        }
        return build(preorder, 0, preorder.length-1, inorder, 0, inorder.length-1, map);
    }

    TreeNode build(int[] preorder, int preStart, int preEnd, int[] inorder, int inStart, int inEnd, HashMap<Integer, Integer> map){
        if(preStart > preEnd || inStart > inEnd) return null;
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[preStart]);
        int index = map.get(root.data);
        int numLeft = index - inStart; // 1
        int numRight = inEnd - index;  // 3
        // preorder = [3, 9, 20, 15, 7] , inorder = [9, 3, 15, 20, 7] 20->3 preStart=2 preEnd=4 inStart=2 inEnd=4
        root.left = build(preorder, preStart+1, preEnd-numRight, inorder, inStart, index-1, map);
        root.right = build(preorder, preStart+numLeft+1, preEnd, inorder, index+1, inEnd, map);

        return root;
    }
}
```

